⟦P0⟧Tutorial: Getting started with fuzzing⟦P1⟧This tutorial introduces the basics of fuzzing in Go. With fuzzing, random data⟦P2⟧is run against your test in an attempt to find vulnerabilities or crash-causing⟦P3⟧inputs. Some examples of vulnerabilities that can be found by fuzzing are ⟦P4⟧⟦P5⟧injection, buffer overflow, denial of service and cross-site scripting attacks.⟦P6⟧In this tutorial, you'll write a fuzz test for a simple function, run the go⟦P7⟧command, and debug and fix issues in the code.⟦P8⟧For help with terminology throughout this tutorial, see the ⟦P9⟧Go Fuzzing⟦P10⟧glossary⟦P11⟧.⟦P12⟧You'll progress through the following sections:⟦P13⟧⟦P14⟧Create a folder for your code.⟦P15⟧⟦P16⟧⟦P17⟧Add code to test.⟦P18⟧⟦P19⟧⟦P20⟧Add a unit test.⟦P21⟧⟦P22⟧⟦P23⟧Add a fuzz test.⟦P24⟧⟦P25⟧⟦P26⟧Fix two bugs.⟦P27⟧⟦P28⟧⟦P29⟧Explore additional resources.⟦P30⟧⟦P31⟧⟦P32⟧Note:⟦P33⟧ For other tutorials, see ⟦P34⟧Tutorials⟦P35⟧.⟦P36⟧⟦P37⟧Note:⟦P38⟧ Go fuzzing currently supports a subset of built-in types, listed in⟦P39⟧the ⟦P40⟧Go Fuzzing docs⟦P41⟧, with support for more built-in⟦P42⟧types to be added in the future.⟦P43⟧Prerequisites⟦P44⟧⟦P45⟧Go.⟦P46⟧ We recommend using the latest version of Go to follow this tutorial.⟦P47⟧For installation instructions, see ⟦P48⟧Installing Go⟦P49⟧.⟦P50⟧⟦P51⟧A tool to edit your code.⟦P52⟧ Any text editor you have will work fine.⟦P53⟧⟦P54⟧A command terminal.⟦P55⟧ Go works well using any terminal on Linux and Mac, and⟦P56⟧on ⟦P57⟧ or cmd in Windows.⟦P58⟧⟦P59⟧An environment that supports fuzzing.⟦P60⟧ Go fuzzing with coverage⟦P61⟧instrumentation is only available on ⟦P62⟧ and ⟦P63⟧ architectures currently.⟦P64⟧Create a folder for your code ⟦P65⟧⟦P66⟧To begin, create a folder for the code you’ll write.⟦P67⟧Open a command prompt and change to your home directory.⟦P68⟧On Linux or Mac:⟦P69⟧On Windows:⟦P70⟧The rest of the tutorial will show a $ as the prompt. The commands you use⟦P71⟧will work on Windows too.⟦P72⟧From the command prompt, create a directory for your code called fuzz.⟦P73⟧Create a module to hold your code.⟦P74⟧Run the ⟦P75⟧ command, giving it your new code’s module path.⟦P76⟧⟦P77⟧Note:⟦P78⟧ For production code, you’d specify a module path that’s more⟦P79⟧specific to your own needs. For more, be sure to see ⟦P80⟧Managing⟦P81⟧dependencies⟦P82⟧.⟦P83⟧Next, you'll add some simple code to reverse a string, which we’ll fuzz later.⟦P84⟧Add code to test ⟦P85⟧⟦P86⟧In this step, you’ll add a function to reverse a string.⟦P87⟧Write the code⟦P88⟧Using your text editor, create a file called ⟦P89⟧ in the fuzz directory.⟦P90⟧Into ⟦P91⟧, at the top of the file, paste the following package⟦P92⟧declaration.⟦P93⟧A standalone program (as opposed to a library) is always in package ⟦P94⟧.⟦P95⟧Beneath the package declaration, paste the following function declaration.⟦P96⟧This function will accept a ⟦P97⟧, loop over it a ⟦P98⟧ at a time, and⟦P99⟧return the reversed string at the end.⟦P100⟧⟦P101⟧Note:⟦P102⟧ This code is based on the ⟦P103⟧ function within⟦P104⟧⟦P105⟧.⟦P106⟧At the top of ⟦P107⟧, beneath the package declaration, paste the following⟦P108⟧⟦P109⟧ function to initialize a string, reverse it, print the output, and⟦P110⟧repeat.⟦P111⟧This function will run a few ⟦P112⟧ operations, then print the output to⟦P113⟧the command line. This can be helpful for seeing the code in action, and⟦P114⟧potentially for debugging.⟦P115⟧The ⟦P116⟧ function uses the fmt package, so you will need to import it.⟦P117⟧The first lines of code should look like this:⟦P118⟧Run the code⟦P119⟧From the command line in the directory containing ⟦P120⟧, run the code.⟦P121⟧You can see the original string, the result of reversing it, then the result of⟦P122⟧reversing it again, which is equivalent to the original.⟦P123⟧Now that the code is running, it’s time to test it.⟦P124⟧Add a unit test ⟦P125⟧⟦P126⟧In this step, you will write a basic unit test for the ⟦P127⟧ function.⟦P128⟧Write the code⟦P129⟧Using your text editor, create a file called ⟦P130⟧ in the fuzz⟦P131⟧directory.⟦P132⟧Paste the following code into ⟦P133⟧.⟦P134⟧This simple test will assert that the listed input strings will be correctly⟦P135⟧reversed.⟦P136⟧Run the code⟦P137⟧Run the unit test using ⟦P138⟧⟦P139⟧Next, you will change the unit test into a fuzz test.⟦P140⟧Add a fuzz test ⟦P141⟧⟦P142⟧The unit test has limitations, namely that each input must be added to the test⟦P143⟧by the developer. One benefit of fuzzing is that it comes up with inputs for⟦P144⟧your code, and may identify edge cases that the test cases you came up with⟦P145⟧didn’t reach.⟦P146⟧In this section you will convert the unit test to a fuzz test so that you can⟦P147⟧generate more inputs with less work!⟦P148⟧Note that you can keep unit tests, benchmarks, and fuzz tests in the same⟦P149⟧⟦P150⟧⟦P151⟧⟦P152⟧ file, but for this example you will convert the unit test to a fuzz⟦P153⟧test.⟦P154⟧Write the code⟦P155⟧In your text editor, replace the unit test in ⟦P156⟧ with the following⟦P157⟧fuzz test.⟦P158⟧Use ⟦P159⟧.⟦P160⟧ to provide a seed corpus⟦P161⟧Fuzzing has a few limitations as well. In your unit test, you could predict the⟦P162⟧expected output of the ⟦P163⟧ function, and verify that the actual output met⟦P164⟧those expectations.⟦P165⟧For example, in the test case ⟦P166⟧ the unit test specifies⟦P167⟧the return as ⟦P168⟧.⟦P169⟧When fuzzing, you can't predict the expected output, since you don't have⟦P170⟧control over the inputs.⟦P171⟧However, there are a few properties of the ⟦P172⟧ function that you can⟦P173⟧verify in a fuzz test. The two properties being checked in this fuzz test are:⟦P174⟧Reversing a string twice preserves the original value⟦P175⟧The reversed string preserves its state as valid ⟦P176⟧.⟦P177⟧Note the syntax differences between the unit test and the fuzz test:⟦P178⟧The function begins with ⟦P179⟧ instead of ⟦P180⟧, and takes ⟦P181⟧⟦P182⟧instead of ⟦P183⟧⟦P184⟧Where you would expect to see a ⟦P185⟧ execution, you instead see ⟦P186⟧⟦P187⟧which takes a fuzz target function whose parameters are ⟦P188⟧ and the⟦P189⟧types to be fuzzed. The inputs from your unit test are provided as seed corpus⟦P190⟧inputs using ⟦P191⟧.⟦P192⟧Ensure the new package, ⟦P193⟧ has been imported.⟦P194⟧With the unit test converted to a fuzz test, it’s time to run the test again.⟦P195⟧Run the code⟦P196⟧Run the fuzz test without fuzzing it to make sure the seed inputs pass.⟦P197⟧You can also run ⟦P198⟧ if you have other tests in that⟦P199⟧file, and you only wish to run the fuzz test.⟦P200⟧Run ⟦P201⟧ with fuzzing, to see if any randomly generated string⟦P202⟧inputs will cause a failure. This is executed using ⟦P203⟧ with a new⟦P204⟧flag, ⟦P205⟧, set to the parameter ⟦P206⟧. Copy the command below.⟦P207⟧Another useful flag is ⟦P208⟧, which restricts the time fuzzing takes.⟦P209⟧For example, specifying ⟦P210⟧ in the test below would mean that,⟦P211⟧as long as no failures occurred earlier, the test would exit by default⟦P212⟧after 10 seconds had elapsed. See ⟦P213⟧this⟦P214⟧section⟦P215⟧ of the ⟦P216⟧⟦P217⟧documentation to see other testing flags.⟦P218⟧Now, run the command you just copied.⟦P219⟧A failure occurred while fuzzing, and the input that caused the problem is⟦P220⟧written to a seed corpus file that will be run the next time ⟦P221⟧ is⟦P222⟧called, even without the ⟦P223⟧ flag. To view the input that caused the⟦P224⟧failure, open the corpus file written to the ⟦P225⟧⟦P226⟧directory in a text editor. Your seed corpus file may contain a different⟦P227⟧string, but the format will be the same.⟦P228⟧The first line of the corpus file indicates the encoding version. Each⟦P229⟧following line represents the value of each type making up the corpus entry.⟦P230⟧Since the fuzz target only takes 1 input, there is only 1 value after the⟦P231⟧version.⟦P232⟧Run ⟦P233⟧ again without the⟦P234⟧ flag; the new failing seed corpus⟦P235⟧entry will be used:⟦P236⟧Since our test has failed, it’s time to debug.⟦P237⟧Fix the invalid string error ⟦P238⟧⟦P239⟧In this section, you will debug the failure, and fix the bug.⟦P240⟧Feel free to spend some time thinking about this and trying to fix the issue⟦P241⟧yourself before moving on.⟦P242⟧Diagnose the error⟦P243⟧There are a few different ways you could debug this error. If you are using ⟦P244⟧⟦P245⟧Code as your text editor, you can ⟦P246⟧set up your⟦P247⟧debugger⟦P248⟧ to⟦P249⟧investigate.⟦P250⟧In this tutorial, we will log useful debugging info to your terminal.⟦P251⟧First, consider the docs for⟦P252⟧⟦P253⟧⟦P254⟧⟦P255⟧.⟦P256⟧The current ⟦P257⟧ function reverses the string byte-by-byte, and therein⟦P258⟧lies our problem. In order to preserve the UTF-8-encoded runes of the original⟦P259⟧string, we must instead reverse the string rune-by-rune.⟦P260⟧To examine why the input (in this case, the Chinese character ⟦P261⟧) is causing⟦P262⟧⟦P263⟧ to produce an invalid string when reversed, you can inspect the number⟦P264⟧of runes in the reversed string.⟦P265⟧Write the code⟦P266⟧In your text editor, replace the fuzz target within ⟦P267⟧ with the⟦P268⟧following.⟦P269⟧This ⟦P270⟧ line will print to the command line if an error occurs, or if⟦P271⟧executing the test with ⟦P272⟧, which can help you debug this particular issue.⟦P273⟧Run the code⟦P274⟧Run the test using go test⟦P275⟧The entire seed corpus used strings in which every character was a single byte.⟦P276⟧However, characters such as 泃 can require several bytes. Thus, reversing the⟦P277⟧string byte-by-byte will invalidate multi-byte characters.⟦P278⟧⟦P279⟧Note:⟦P280⟧ If you’re curious about how Go deals with strings, read the blog post⟦P281⟧⟦P282⟧Strings, bytes, runes and characters in Go⟦P283⟧ for a⟦P284⟧deeper understanding.⟦P285⟧With a better understanding of the bug, correct the error in the ⟦P286⟧⟦P287⟧function.⟦P288⟧Fix the error⟦P289⟧To correct the ⟦P290⟧ function, let’s traverse the string by runes, instead⟦P291⟧of by bytes.⟦P292⟧Write the code⟦P293⟧In your text editor, replace the existing ⟦P294⟧() function with the following.⟦P295⟧The key difference is that ⟦P296⟧ is now iterating over each ⟦P297⟧ in the⟦P298⟧string, rather than each ⟦P299⟧. Note that this is just an example, and does not⟦P300⟧handle ⟦P301⟧combining characters⟦P302⟧ correctly.⟦P303⟧Run the code⟦P304⟧Run the test using ⟦P305⟧⟦P306⟧The test now passes!⟦P307⟧Fuzz it again with ⟦P308⟧, to see if there are any new bugs.⟦P309⟧We can see that the string is different from the original after being⟦P310⟧reversed twice. This time the input itself is invalid unicode. How is this⟦P311⟧possible if we’re fuzzing with strings?⟦P312⟧Let’s debug again.⟦P313⟧Fix the double reverse error ⟦P314⟧⟦P315⟧In this section, you will debug the double reverse failure and fix the bug.⟦P316⟧Feel free to spend some time thinking about this and trying to fix the issue⟦P317⟧yourself before moving on.⟦P318⟧Diagnose the error⟦P319⟧Like before, there are several ways you could debug this failure. In this case,⟦P320⟧using a⟦P321⟧⟦P322⟧debugger⟦P323⟧⟦P324⟧would be a great approach.⟦P325⟧In this tutorial, we will log useful debugging info in the ⟦P326⟧ function.⟦P327⟧Look closely at the reversed string to spot the error. In Go, ⟦P328⟧a string is a⟦P329⟧read only slice of bytes⟦P330⟧, and can contain bytes⟦P331⟧that aren’t valid ⟦P332⟧. The original string is a byte slice with one byte,⟦P333⟧⟦P334⟧. When the input string is set to ⟦P335⟧, Go encodes the byte slice to⟦P336⟧⟦P337⟧, and replaces the byte with the ⟦P338⟧ character �. When we compare the⟦P339⟧replacement ⟦P340⟧ character to the input byte slice, they are clearly not equal.⟦P341⟧Write the code⟦P342⟧In your text editor, replace the ⟦P343⟧ function with the following.⟦P344⟧This will help us understand what is going wrong when converting the string⟦P345⟧to a slice of runes.⟦P346⟧Run the code⟦P347⟧This time, we only want to run the failing test in order to inspect the logs. To⟦P348⟧do this, we will use ⟦P349⟧.⟦P350⟧To run a specific corpus entry within ⟦P351⟧, you can provide⟦P352⟧{⟦P353⟧}⟦P354⟧{filename} to ⟦P355⟧. This can be helpful when debugging.⟦P356⟧In this case, set the ⟦P357⟧ flag equal to the exact hash of the failing test.⟦P358⟧Copy and paste the unique hash from your terminal;⟦P359⟧it will be different than the one below.⟦P360⟧Knowing that the input is invalid unicode, let’s fix the error in our ⟦P361⟧⟦P362⟧function.⟦P363⟧Fix the error⟦P364⟧To fix this issue, let's return an error if the input to ⟦P365⟧ isn't valid⟦P366⟧⟦P367⟧.⟦P368⟧Write the code⟦P369⟧In your text editor, replace the existing ⟦P370⟧ function with the⟦P371⟧following.⟦P372⟧This change will return an error if the input string contains characters⟦P373⟧which are not valid ⟦P374⟧.⟦P375⟧Since the Reverse function now returns an error, modify the ⟦P376⟧ function to⟦P377⟧discard the extra error value. Replace the existing ⟦P378⟧ function with the⟦P379⟧following.⟦P380⟧These calls to ⟦P381⟧ should return a nil error, since the input⟦P382⟧string is valid ⟦P383⟧.⟦P384⟧You will need to import the errors and the ⟦P385⟧ packages.⟦P386⟧The import statement in ⟦P387⟧ should look like the following.⟦P388⟧Modify the ⟦P389⟧ file to check for errors and skip the test if⟦P390⟧errors are generated by returning.⟦P391⟧Use ⟦P392⟧.⟦P393⟧ to provide a seed corpus⟦P394⟧Rather than returning, you can also call ⟦P395⟧ to stop the execution of⟦P396⟧that fuzz input.⟦P397⟧Run the code⟦P398⟧Run the test using go test⟦P399⟧Fuzz it with ⟦P400⟧, then after a few seconds has passed, stop⟦P401⟧fuzzing with ⟦P402⟧. The fuzz test will run until it encounters a failing⟦P403⟧input unless you pass the ⟦P404⟧ flag. The default is to run forever if no⟦P405⟧failures occur, and the process can be interrupted with ⟦P406⟧.⟦P407⟧Fuzz it with ⟦P408⟧ which will fuzz for 30⟦P409⟧seconds before exiting if no failure was found.⟦P410⟧Fuzzing passed!⟦P411⟧In addition to the ⟦P412⟧ flag, several new flags have been added to ⟦P413⟧ and can be viewed in the ⟦P414⟧documentation⟦P415⟧.⟦P416⟧See ⟦P417⟧Go Fuzzing⟦P418⟧ for more⟦P419⟧information on terms used in fuzzing output. For example, "new interesting"⟦P420⟧refers to inputs that expand the code coverage of the existing fuzz test⟦P421⟧corpus. The number of "new interesting" inputs can be expected to increase⟦P422⟧sharply as fuzzing begins, spike several times as new code paths are⟦P423⟧discovered, then taper off over time.⟦P424⟧Conclusion ⟦P425⟧⟦P426⟧Nicely done! You've just introduced yourself to fuzzing in Go.⟦P427⟧The next step is to choose a function in your code that you'd like to fuzz, and⟦P428⟧try it out! If fuzzing finds a bug in your code, consider adding it to the⟦P429⟧⟦P430⟧trophy case⟦P431⟧.⟦P432⟧If you experience any problems or have an idea for a feature, ⟦P433⟧file an⟦P434⟧issue⟦P435⟧.⟦P436⟧For discussion and general feedback about the feature, you can also participate⟦P437⟧in the ⟦P438⟧#fuzzing channel⟦P439⟧ in⟦P440⟧Gophers Slack.⟦P441⟧Check out the documentation at ⟦P442⟧⟦P443⟧⟦P444⟧ for⟦P445⟧further reading.⟦P446⟧Completed code⟦P447⟧Use ⟦P448⟧.⟦P449⟧ to provide a seed corpus⟦P450⟧⟦P451⟧Back to top⟦P452⟧⟦P453⟧
